<!--
  /why — the case for SUI, built from the architecture. Spine: Ship needs
  correct custom UI fast from agents; the bottleneck is decisions and
  correctness, not reactivity; so remove decisions (standard library +
  enforced design system + narrow grammar) and tighten the loop. Competitive
  machinery, agent-first priorities. Do not name competitors; do not dunk.
-->
<div class="doc why-doc">
  <p class="eyebrow">Introduction</p>
  <h1>Why SUI</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Ship exists to build custom applications fast — and, increasingly, to
    build them with agents. SUI is Ship's UI layer, and every decision in it
    follows from that one requirement: an agent, handed a spec, should produce
    a working, correct, on-brand interface without a person babysitting a
    thousand small choices.
  </p>
  <p>
    SUI is a compiler-based reactive UI framework. On syntax and performance it
    sits with the modern compilers — signals, fine-grained updates, compilation
    to standard custom elements with shadow-DOM-scoped styles. What's different
    isn't the machinery; it's the priorities. SUI is built to be the most
    ergonomic framework for an <em>agent</em> to write — not the most ergonomic
    for a human to hand-author. Those aren't the same target, and nearly
    everything else optimizes for the second.
  </p>

  <h2>What "ergonomic for an agent" means</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Tight feedback loops.</strong> The compiler, the language server,
    and a strict linter give instant, precise ground truth on every edit. An
    unknown token, a hand-rolled layout that should be a primitive, a value off
    the spacing scale — the model learns it's wrong the moment it writes it,
    from the same analysis a human editor surfaces, not from a broken render
    three steps later.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Batteries included.</strong> A large standard library — around sixty
    designed, accessible primitives, plus packs for charts, media, code, and
    canvas — means the component already exists. The agent composes a
    <code>sui-combobox</code>; it doesn't rebuild one from <code>div</code>s and
    get the keyboard handling subtly wrong. Data and server rendering come with
    the stack — that's <a href="/deck">deck</a>'s half: <code>data live</code>
    yields a server-rendered value that hydrates and updates over a live stream,
    with no plumbing to author.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Strict opinions.</strong> The language is narrow on purpose — one way
    to declare a prop, bind a value, loop — and the design system is enforced by
    the compiler, where off-system styling is a build error, not a review note.
    The smaller the space of valid programs, the less there is to get wrong and
    the more the tooling can guarantee.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is the right shape</h2>
  <p>
    The hard part of agent-authored UI was never reactivity. It's decisions and
    correctness. A model building a screen makes hundreds of small choices —
    color, spacing, focus behavior, whether that dropdown is genuinely
    accessible — and gets them confidently, plausibly wrong. Hand it more
    freedom and more primitives and you only widen the surface to be wrong on.
    SUI goes the other way: the standard library makes the components a given,
    the design system makes the styling a given, the strict grammar makes the
    shape a given. What's left for the model is the part that actually needs
    intelligence — composition and logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    And it stays on the platform: html, ts, and css compiling to standard custom
    elements, no parallel universe to learn for a person or a model.
    <a href="/deck">deck</a> wraps it with routing, server rendering, bundling,
    and live data, so an application is a full stack rather than a pile of view
    components.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest version</h2>
  <p>
    SUI is the same kind of tool as the compilers it's measured against, with a
    different set of priorities. It optimizes for tight loops, batteries, and
    strict opinions — for a machine writing UI and a human steering it — and it
    will feel constrained if you came looking for a flexible toolkit to
    hand-write. That constraint is the product. It's what turns "an agent built
    this" from a warning into a guarantee.
  </p>
</div>
